-- title:
--   Extracting every bit of a valid subset mask
-- statement:
--   Let $m<2^L$ be a natural mask, and let $D,F$ be output and scratch addresses with $D+L\le F$ and $F+L+1\le\mathrm{space}\le\mathrm{word}$. Assume $2^{L+1}\le\mathrm{word}$, $d+L+1\le\mathrm{depth}$, and a program containing the bit-row body, power-table body and recursive mask-clearing body.
--
--   The call with $(L,m,D,F)$ terminates within $100L^2+127L+75$ steps and yields
--   $$
--   \nu(D+j)=\operatorname{bit}_j(m)\quad(j<L),\qquad
--   \nu(F+i)=2^i\quad(i\le L).
--   $$
--   Here $\operatorname{bit}_j(m)$ is zero when clearing coordinate $j$ leaves $m$ unchanged, and one otherwise. Memory below $F$ outside $[D,D+L)$ is preserved.
--
--   This constructs the Boolean coordinate row used in local mask tests.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/c0ldzy17/three-sum-lean/blob/2b639655e07421b9ac743de9090459bab473a7c0/Submission/ThreeSum/LightMaskBits.lean#L42-L113

